eference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solutions"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d413340f87858f3f84e25b85893b350a2e8be83892d0b2efbd0f6ed40f0c950e" "basis" 575 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solutions"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d413340f87858f3f84e25b85893b350a2e8be83892d0b2efbd0f6ed40f0c950e" "basis" 575 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2{A}^{2}+4A}.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d413340f87858f3f84e25b85893b350a2e8be83892d0b2efbd0f6ed40f0c950e" "basis" 575 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2A,\\,A+2,\\,2A(A+2))=2A(A+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $A=0$ and $A=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2} & =\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ 2A(A+2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}) & =2A(A+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 2A(A+2)}{2A}+\\frac{4A(A+2)}{A+2} & =\\frac{2A(A+2)}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2A}(A+2)}{\\cancel{2A}}+\\frac{4A\\cancel{(A+2)}}{\\cancel{A+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}}{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}} \\\\ 5(A+2)+4A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(A+2)+4A & =1 \\\\ 5A+10+4A & =1 \\\\ 9A & =-9 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-1\\ne 0$ and $-1\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $A=-1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $A=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d413340f87858f3f84e25b85893b350a2e8be83892d0b2efbd0f6ed40f0c950e" "basis" 575 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c8bf30b3dab6ab2a29a28e3a4abc89fa30fa33ebb06faee6b75a921ac589fb4" "basis" 575 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d413340f87858f3f84e25b85893b350a2e8be83892d0b2efbd0f6ed40f0c950e" "basis" 575 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947355/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-276460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{3}{5s}=\\frac{1}{s+1}.$\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(5s(s+1),\\,5s,\\,s+1)=5s(s+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $s=0$ and $s=-1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{3}{5s} & =\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ 5s(s+1)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{3}{5s}) & =5s(s+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ \\frac{20s(s+1)}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{15s(s+1)}{5s} & =\\frac{5s(s+1)}{s+1} \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{5s(s+1)}}{\\cancel{5s(s+1)}}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{5s}(s+1)}{\\cancel{5s}} & =\\frac{5s\\cancel{(s+1)}}{\\cancel{s+1}} \\\\ 4+3(s+1) & =5s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+3(s+1) & =5s \\\\ 4+3s+3 & =5s \\\\ 7 & =2s \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{7}{2}\\ne 0$ and $\\frac{7}{2}\\ne -1,$ we conclude that $s=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $s=\\frac{7}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-276460" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276460" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}=\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)}.$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3h+2,\\,5h,\\,h(3h+2))=5h(3h+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h} & =\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ 5h(3h+2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}) & =5h(3h+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5h(3h+2)}{3h+2}+\\frac{5h(3h+2)}{5h} & =\\frac{5h(3h+2)}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5h\\cancel{(3h+2)}}{\\cancel{3h+2}}+\\frac{\\cancel{5h}(3h+2)}{\\cancel{5h}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{h(3h+2)}}{\\cancel{h(3h+2)}} \\\\ 5h+(3h+2) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5h+(3h+2) & =5 \\\\ 5h+3h+2 & =5 \\\\ 8h & =3 \\\\ h & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{8}\\ne 0$ and $\\frac{3}{8}\\ne -\\frac{2}{3},$ we conclude that $h=\\frac{3}{8}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $h=\\frac{3}{8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-276457" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276457" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-3519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{2}{z}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-3519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z,\\,z-1,\\,z(z-1))=z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2}{z} \\\\ z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)}) & =z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{z} \\\\ \\frac{3z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{3z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{z} \\\\ \\frac{3z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{z}} \\\\ 3z-3 & =2z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-3 & =2z-2 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-3519" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-3519" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-3519" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=-1"] ["math" "z=-2"] ["math" "z=1"] ["math" "z=3"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-3519" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solutions"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}=\\frac{2}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n+4,\\,n(n+4),\\,n)=n(n+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $n=-4$ and $n=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2}{n} \\\\ n(n+4)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}) & =n(n+4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n(n+4)}{n+4}-\\frac{5n(n+4)}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2n(n+4)}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n\\cdot \\cancel{(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n+4}}-\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{n(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n(n+4)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot (n+4)}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ 3n-5 & =2(n+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-5 & =2(n+4) \\\\ 3n-5 & =2n+8 \\\\ n & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $13\\ne 0$ and $13\\ne -4,$ we conclude that $n=13$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $n=13.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-276459" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276459" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{3x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2x,\\,3x(x+3),\\,x+3)=6x(x+3).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=0$ and $x=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{3x(x+3)} & =\\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ 6x(x+3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{3x(x+3)}) & =6x(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{6x(x+3)}{2x}+\\frac{6x(x+3)}{3x(x+3)} & =\\frac{6x(x+3)}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{2x}(x+3)}{\\cancel{2x}}+\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{3x(x+3)}}{\\cancel{3x(x+3)}} & =\\frac{6x\\cancel{(x+3)}}{\\cancel{x+3}} \\\\ 3(x+3)+2 & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)+2 & =6x \\\\ 3x+9+2 & =6x \\\\ 11 & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{11}{3}\\ne 0$ and $\\frac{11}{3}\\ne -3,$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $x=\\frac{11}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-276458" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276458" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(V+4),\\,V-4,\\,(V+4)(V-4))=2(V+4)(V-4).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=-4$ and $V=4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4} & =\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ 2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}) & =2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot \\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{V-4} & =\\frac{16(V+4)(V-4)}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(V+4)}(V-4)}{\\cancel{2(V+4)}}+\\frac{2(V+4)\\cancel{(V-4)}}{\\cancel{V-4}} & =\\frac{16\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}}{\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}} \\\\ (V-4)+2(V+4) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(V-4)+2(V+4) & =16 \\\\ V-4+2V+8 & =16 \\\\ 3V & =12 \\\\ V & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $V=4$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "V=2"] ["math" "V=3"] ["math" "V=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "V=\\frac{3}{4}"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solutions"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y+2}=\\frac{7}{{y}^{2}-4}-\\frac{1}{y-2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+2}=\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+2,\\,y-2,\\,(y-2)(y+2))=(y-2)(y+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+2} & =\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2} \\\\ (y-2)(y+2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{y+2} & =(y-2)(y+2)\\cdot (\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2}) \\\\ \\frac{3(y-2)(y+2)}{y+2} & =\\frac{7(y-2)(y+2)}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{(y-2)(y+2)}{y-2} \\\\ \\frac{3(y-2)\\cancel{(y+2)}}{\\cancel{y+2}} & =\\frac{7\\cancel{(y-2)(y+2)}}{\\cancel{(y-2)(y+2)}}-\\frac{\\cancel{(y-2)}(y+2)}{\\cancel{y-2}} \\\\ 3(y-2) & =7-(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(y-2) & =7-(y+2) \\\\ 3y-6 & =7-y-2 \\\\ 4y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\frac{11}{4}\\ne -2$ and $\\frac{11}{4}\\ne 2,$ we conclude that $y=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $y=\\frac{11}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-276549" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276549" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-14810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation$\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3x+2}-\\frac{2}{x+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-14810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x+1,\\,x+2,\\,(x+1)(x+2))=(x+1)(x+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=-1$ and $x=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ (x+1)(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2} & =(x+1)(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2}{x+1}) \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{x+2} & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2(x+1)(x+2)}{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)(x+2)}}{\\cancel{(x+1)(x+2)}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)}\\cdot (x+2)}{\\cancel{x+1}} \\\\ x+1 & =1-2(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =1-2(x+2) \\\\ x+1 & =1-2x-4 \\\\ 3x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-\\frac{4}{3}\\ne -1$ and $-\\frac{4}{3}\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $x=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $x=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-14810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-14810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-14810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{3}{4}"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-14810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+4,\\,m-3,\\,(m+4)(m-3))=(m+4)(m-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-4$ and $m=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3} & =\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ (m+4)(m-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}) & =(m+4)(m-3)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{m+4}+\\frac{(m+4)(m-3)}{m-3} & =\\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)}(m-3)}{\\cancel{m+4}}+\\frac{(m+4)\\cancel{(m-3)}}{\\cancel{m-3}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}}{\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}} \\\\ 3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3 \\\\ 4m-5 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =8 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne -4$ and $2\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $m=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-117687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "m=-6"] ["math" "m=1"] ["math" "m=2"] ["math" "m=\\frac{7}{2}"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "m=2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}-25}=\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}.$\n\n$w=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)}=\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w-5,\\,w+5,\\,(w-5)(w+5))=(w-5)(w+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=-5$ and $w=5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5} \\\\ (w-5)(w+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =(w-5)(w+5)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}) \\\\ \\frac{(w-5)(w+5)}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =\\frac{(w-5)(w+5)}{w-5}-\\frac{4(w-5)(w+5)}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(w-5)(w+5)}}{\\cancel{(w-5)(w+5)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(w-5)}(w+5)}{\\cancel{(w-5)}}-\\frac{4(w-5)\\cancel{(w+5)}}{\\cancel{w+5}} \\\\ 1 & =(w+5)-4(w-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(w+5)-4(w-5) \\\\ 1 & =w+5-4w+20 \\\\ 3w & =24 \\\\ w & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $8\\ne -5$ and $8\\ne 5,$ we conclude that $w=8$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $w=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-276552" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276552" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2{y}^{2}+10y}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+5,\\,2y,\\,2y(y+5))=2y(y+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=0$ and $y=-5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y} & =\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ 2y(y+5)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}) & =2y(y+5)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y(y+5)}{y+5}+\\frac{2y(y+5)}{2y} & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2y(y+5)}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y\\cancel{(y+5)}}{\\cancel{y+5}}+\\frac{\\cancel{2y}(y+5)}{\\cancel{2y}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2y(y+5)}}{\\cancel{2y(y+5)}} \\\\ 6y+(y+5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+(y+5) & =5 \\\\ 7y+5 & =5 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $y=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-117631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=2"] ["math" "y=5"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-117631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solutions"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}+6h}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(h,\\,2h(h+3),\\,h+3)=2h(h+3).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ 2h(h+3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}) & =2h(h+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2h(h+3)}{h}-\\frac{2h(h+3)}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{2h(h+3)}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{h}\\cdot (h+3)}{\\cancel{h}}-\\frac{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}}{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}} & =\\frac{2h\\cdot \\cancel{(h+3)}}{\\cancel{h+3}} \\\\ 2(h+3)-1 & =2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(h+3)-1 & =2h \\\\ 2h+6-1 & =2h \\\\ 5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above resulted in a false statement, our equation has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-118179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "h=-2"] ["math" "h=-4"] ["math" "h=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "h=\\frac{5}{3}"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solutions"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2{A}^{2}+4A}.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2A,\\,A+2,\\,2A(A+2))=2A(A+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $A=0$ and $A=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2} & =\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ 2A(A+2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}) & =2A(A+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 2A(A+2)}{2A}+\\frac{4A(A+2)}{A+2} & =\\frac{2A(A+2)}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2A}(A+2)}{\\cancel{2A}}+\\frac{4A\\cancel{(A+2)}}{\\cancel{A+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}}{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}} \\\\ 5(A+2)+4A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(A+2)+4A & =1 \\\\ 5A+10+4A & =1 \\\\ 9A & =-9 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-1\\ne 0$ and $-1\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $A=-1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $A=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-276464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nWe make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z-1,\\,2z,\\,z(z-1))=2z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ 2z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}) & =2z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{10z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ 10z-10 & =z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10z-10 & =z-1 \\\\ 9z & =9 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-118123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=-2"] ["math" "z=3"] ["math" "z=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "z=\\frac{3}{2}"] ["text" "No solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-118123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No solutions"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-270927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{{B}^{2}+2B}=\\frac{2}{B}.$\n\n$B=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-270927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{B(B+2)}=\\frac{2}{B}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(B+2,\\,B(B+2),\\,B)=B(B+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $B=0$ and $B=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{B(B+2)} & =\\frac{2}{B} \\\\ B(B+2)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{B(B+2)}) & =B(B+2)\\cdot \\frac{2}{B} \\\\ \\frac{3B(B+2)}{B+2}-\\frac{B(B+2)}{B(B+2)} & =\\frac{2B(B+2)}{B} \\\\ \\frac{3B\\cdot \\cancel{(B+2)}}{\\cancel{B+2}}-\\frac{\\cancel{B(B+2)}}{\\cancel{B(B+2)}} & =\\frac{2\\cancel{B}\\cdot (B+2)}{\\cancel{B}} \\\\ 3B-1 & =2(B+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $B,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3B-1 & =2(B+2) \\\\ B & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5\\ne 0$ and $5\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $B=5$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $B=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-270927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daf45ff5463bb1491b40357502e3877022a9b1786fafec8b05ca56e514ee3b60"} {"question" "q-270927" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "q-270927" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/state.json" "file_sha256" "fca5e8ff6240b61ea02c0e079ef63068a523835c79abbcd801031a5fec22f14a"} {"question" "e-9367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/example-9367.json" "file_sha256" "d2b8fec6e905321a5ef5070d703f4b53318e8d996045207be9fe4c920c0ab60e"} {"question" "e-9367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ x(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)}) & =x(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x(x-2)}{x-2}-\\frac{8x(x-2)}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3x(x-2)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}}-\\frac{8\\cancel{x(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x(x-2)}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{x}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 4x-8 & =3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ 4x-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ 4x-3x & =-6+8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/example-9367.json" "file_sha256" "d2b8fec6e905321a5ef5070d703f4b53318e8d996045207be9fe4c920c0ab60e"} {"question" "e-5824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/example-5824.json" "file_sha256" "baa4259d2720f27941fb2143abb39108559a15c9ac8ad7da039a4e4ab33408b8"} {"question" "e-5824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2} & =\\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ (k-2)(k-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2}) & =(k-2)(k-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)(k-3)}{k-3}-\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{k-2} & =\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k-3)}}{\\cancel{k-3}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)}\\cdot (k-3)}{\\cancel{k-2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}}{\\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}} \\\\ 3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2 \\\\ 3k-6-2k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/example-5824.json" "file_sha256" "baa4259d2720f27941fb2143abb39108559a15c9ac8ad7da039a4e4ab33408b8"} {"question" "e-5822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/example-5822.json" "file_sha256" "45d79bd9b1d9194f45aec25b3efde283099856cce065bcc776125d5b9ad6c6b0"} {"question" "e-5822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u}  =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( 3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u} & =\\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ 3u(u+1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u}) & =3u(u+1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u(u+1)}{u+1}-\\frac{3u(u+1)}{3u} & =\\frac{15u(u+1)}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u\\cdot \\cancel{(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3u}\\cdot (u+1)}{\\cancel{3u}} & =\\frac{15\\cdot \\cancel{u(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u(u+1)}} \\\\ 9u-(u+1) & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9u-(u+1) & =15 \\\\ 9u-u-1 & =15 \\\\ 8u & =16 \\\\ u & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\neq -1$ and $2\\neq 0,$ we conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037752/example-5822.json" "file_sha256" "45d79bd9b1d9194f45aec25b3efde283099856cce065bcc776125d5b9ad6c6b0"}] "usage" {"q-3519" [] "q-117631" [] "q-276464" [] "q-117682" [] "e-5822" [] "e-9367" [] "e-5824" [] "q-276459" []} "basis" 1283 "retractions" [[17592186083572 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{z - 1} - (3)/(z(z - 1)) = \\frac{2}{z}$."] [17592186083407 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y + 5} + \\frac{1}{2y} = \\frac{5}{2y^{2} + 10y}$."] [17592186083049 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}$."] [17592186083049 :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2)$.\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2)) &= \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\nx(x - 2) \\cdot (\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2))) &= x(x - 2) \\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3x - 6 \\\\\n4x - 3x &= -6 + 8 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions."] [17592186082958 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}$."] [17592186082958 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3)$.\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2} &= (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2}) &= (k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2 \\cdot (k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2 \\cdot (k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2 \\\\\n3k - 6 - 2k + 6 &= 2 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."] [17592186082957 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}$."] [17592186082957 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1)$.\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u} &= (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n3u(u + 1) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u}) &= 3u(u + 1) \\cdot (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u(u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u(u + 1))/(3u) &= (15u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u \\cdot (u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u \\cdot (u + 1))/(3u) &= (15 \\cdot u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15 \\\\\n9u - u - 1 &= 15 \\\\\n8u &= 16 \\\\\nu &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n2\\neq -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186079773 17592186079774 17592186079775 17592186079776 17592186079777 17592186080799 17592186080800 17592186080801 17592186080802 17592186080803 17592186263432 17592186263433 17592186263434 17592186263435 17592186263436 17592186263471 17592186263472 17592186263479 17592186263480] "immutable_fields" [[17592186079772 :answer-field/correct] [17592186079772 :answer-field/id] [17592186079772 :answer-field/key] [17592186079772 :answer-field/type] [17592186080798 :answer-field/correct] [17592186080798 :answer-field/id] [17592186080798 :answer-field/key] [17592186080798 :answer-field/type] [17592186263431 :answer-field/correct] [17592186263431 :answer-field/id] [17592186263431 :answer-field/key] [17592186263431 :answer-field/type] [17592186263470 :answer-field/correct] [17592186263470 :answer-field/id] [17592186263470 :answer-field/key] [17592186263470 :answer-field/type] [17592186263478 :answer-field/correct] [17592186263478 :answer-field/id] [17592186263478 :answer-field/key] [17592186263478 :answer-field/type]] "no_hi